          "secNumber": "1.9",
          "heading": "Consistency and Asymptotic Behavior of Point Estimators",
          "content": "While unbiasedness and UMVUE optimality focus on exact finite-sample properties, real-world data collection often involves large sample sizes. In asymptotic estimation theory, we investigate how an estimator behaves as the sample size $n$ approaches infinity.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{X}_n = (X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_n)$ be a sequence of random vectors of increasing dimension, and let $T_n = T_n(\\mathbf{X}_n)$ be a sequence of estimators for a parameter $\\tau(\\theta)$.\n\n### Weak Consistency\n\nA sequence of estimators $\\{T_n\\}$ is said to be **weakly consistent** (or simply **consistent**) for $\\tau(\\theta)$ if $T_n$ converges in probability to $\\tau(\\theta)$ as $n \\to \\infty$, under every candidate measure $P_\\theta$:\n\n$$T_n \\xrightarrow{P_\\theta} \\tau(\\theta) \\quad \\forall \\theta \\in \\Theta$$\n\nThat is, for every arbitrarily small tolerance $\\varepsilon > 0$:\n\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} P_\\theta\\left( |T_n - \\tau(\\theta)| \\ge \\varepsilon \\right) = 0 \\quad \\forall \\theta \\in \\Theta$$\n\n### Strong Consistency\n\nA sequence of estimators $\\{T_n\\}$ is said to be **strongly consistent** for $\\tau(\\theta)$ if $T_n$ converges almost surely to $\\tau(\\theta)$ as $n \\to \\infty$:\n\n$$P_\\theta\\left( \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} T_n = \\tau(\\theta) \\right) = 1 \\quad \\forall \\theta \\in \\Theta$$\n\nBy standard measure-theoretic convergence implications, strong consistency strictly implies weak consistency, but the converse is not generally true.\n\n### The Mean Squared Error Criterion for Consistency\n\nA highly useful sufficient condition for consistency is provided by the Mean Squared Error:\n\n#### Theorem:\nIf the Mean Squared Error of a sequence of estimators vanishes as $n \\to \\infty$:\n\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\text{MSE}_\\theta(T_n) = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\mathbb{E}_\\theta\\left[ (T_n - \\tau(\\theta))^2 \\right] = 0 \\quad \\forall \\theta \\in \\Theta$$\n\nthen $T_n$ is weakly consistent for $\\tau(\\theta)$.\n\n#### Proof via Chebyshev's Inequality:\nBy Chebyshev's (or Markov's) inequality applied to the non-negative random variable $(T_n - \\tau(\\theta))^2$:\n\n$$P_\\theta\\left( |T_n - \\tau(\\theta)| \\ge \\varepsilon \\right) = P_\\theta\\left( (T_n - \\tau(\\theta))^2 \\ge \\varepsilon^2 \\right) \\le \\frac{\\mathbb{E}_\\theta\\left[ (T_n - \\tau(\\theta))^2 \\right]}{\\varepsilon^2} = \\frac{\\text{MSE}_\\theta(T_n)}{\\varepsilon^2}$$\n\nTaking the limit as $n \\to \\infty$:\n\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} P_\\theta\\left( |T_n - \\tau(\\theta)| \\ge \\varepsilon \\right) \\le \\frac{1}{\\varepsilon^2} \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\text{MSE}_\\theta(T_n) = 0$$\n\nBecause probabilities are bounded below by zero, the squeeze theorem establishes that the probability limit is zero, proving weak consistency.\n\nUsing the MSE decomposition $\\text{MSE}(T_n) = \\text{Var}(T_n) + [\\text{Bias}(T_n)]^2$, this theorem provides a practical two-step verification for consistency:\n1. Verify that the estimator is asymptotically unbiased: $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\text{Bias}_\\theta(T_n) = 0$.\n2. Verify that its variance shrinks to zero: $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\text{Var}_\\theta(T_n) = 0$.\n\nIf both conditions hold, the estimator is guaranteed to be consistent.",
